L33: arXiv:2602.09541v1 [cs.CV] 10 Feb 2026
L34: # Scalpel: Fine-Grained Alignment of Attention Activation Manifolds via Mixture Gaussian Bridges to Mitigate Multimodal Hallucination
L35: 
L36: Ziqiang Shi^{⋆} ^{†}^{†}thanks: Corresponding author: shiziqiang@fujitsu.com    Rujie Liu^{⋆}    Shanshan Yu^{†}    Satoshi Munakata^{†}    Koichi Shirahata^{†} Affiliation: ^{⋆} Fujitsu Research & Development Center Co.,LTD., Beijing, China Affiliation: ^{†} Fujitsu Limited, Tokyo, Japan
L37: ###### Abstract
L38: Rapid progress in large vision-language models (LVLMs) has achieved unprecedented performance in vision-language tasks. However, due to the strong prior of large language models (LLMs) and misaligned attention across modalities, LVLMs often generate outputs inconsistent with visual content - termed hallucination. To address this, we propose Scalpel, a method that reduces hallucination by refining attention activation distributions toward more credible regions.
L39: Scalpel predicts trusted attention directions for each head in Transformer layers during inference and adjusts activations accordingly. It employs a Gaussian mixture model to capture multi-peak distributions of attention in trust and hallucination manifolds, and uses entropic optimal transport (equivalent to Schrödinger bridge problem) to map Gaussian components precisely.
L40: During mitigation, Scalpel dynamically adjusts intervention strength and direction based on component membership and mapping relationships between hallucination and trust activations. Extensive experiments across multiple datasets and benchmarks demonstrate that Scalpel effectively mitigates hallucinations, outperforming previous methods and achieving state-of-the-art performance. Moreover, Scalpel is model- and data-agnostic, requiring no additional computation, only a single decoding step.
L41: ## 1 Introduction
L42: Large visual language models (LVLMs) have become essential tools for handling diverse visual tasks and performing complex visual question-answering due to their strong capabilities in content understanding and generation [cite22†18 , cite23†33 , cite24†40 , cite25†36 ].
L43: Despite these advancements, LVLMs often suffer from the “hallucination” problem, where generated text appears plausible but misaligns with image content or even fabricates elements not present in the input [cite26†19 , cite27†13 , cite28†27 , cite27†13 , cite29†9 , cite30†37 , cite31†29 ]. This issue significantly undermines their reliability in critical domains such as healthcare, autonomous driving, and security monitoring.
L44: cite32†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Schematic diagram illustrating the principle of the Scalpel method. First, the trusted and hallucinated attention activations of all heads are obtained by inputting both correct and hallucinated data into the LVLM. These activations serve two purposes. The first is to identify the heads with the highest hallucination discrimination capability using a classifier.
L45: The second is to analyze the distribution of credible and hallucinated attention activations across tokens, thereby determining the influence of each individual token on each head.
L46: The hallucinations in current LVLMs are generally attributed to two main factors: the strong language priors inherited from pre-trained large language models (LLMs) [cite26†19 , cite27†13 ], and the model’s tendency to over-attend to irrelevant visual tokens or system tokens unrelated to the given instruction [cite28†27 , cite33†34 , cite30†37 , cite31†29 ].
L47: To mitigate this, researchers have explored reinforcement learning (RL) strategies that fine-tune LVLMs using high-quality [cite26†19 , cite34†20 , cite28†27 , cite35†4 ], hallucination-free feedback—either human-labeled or AI-generated—to improve alignment between vision and language while reducing reliance on LLM priors. However, these methods demand substantial computational resources for annotation and training, making them impractical in resource-constrained environments.
L48: Consequently, recent studies have shifted focus toward inference-time optimization techniques that do not require retraining, such as contrastive decoding [cite27†13 , cite29†9 , cite36†7 , cite37†35 ], which reduces hallucinations by adjusting logit scores. Although training-free, these methods still fall short in effectiveness and often introduce noticeable latency due to multi-step decoding.
L49: While both approaches yield some success, they rarely investigate the internal mechanisms of the LVLM itself to uncover the root causes of hallucinations. In contrast, this paper delves into the role of attention activation across different heads and layers within the LVLM’s Transformer [cite38†31 ] architecture during the generation of hallucinated content. We discover that individual attention heads contribute differently to hallucinations.
L50: By precisely identifying those responsible and applying targeted interventions, we can significantly enhance output quality and multimodal reasoning performance.
L51: Our key contributions are summarized as follows:
L52: 
L53:   * •
L54: 
L55: We propose Scalpel, a novel, training-free, plug-and-play method that enables customized correction of attention activations at the token level without compromising the LLM’s knowledge capacity, effectively reducing hallucinations in LVLMs.
L56: 
L57:   * •
L58: To achieve precise attention correction per token, Scalpel employs Gaussian mixture models (GMMs) to represent trusted and hallucinated attention activation manifolds, then applies Schrödinger bridge problem solving theory to compute optimal transport between these GMMs as shown in Figure cite39†1 . This allows for component-specific correction based on the current token’s activation.
L59: 
L60:   * •
L61: Experimental results on POPE [cite40†16 ] and MME [cite41†8 ] benchmarks demonstrate that Scalpel significantly improves performance on LLaVA-1.5 [cite22†18 ] and Qwen2.5-VL [cite42†1 ], proving its model-agnostic and task-agnostic applicability across multiple databases.
L62: ## 2 Related Work
L63: ### 2.1 Hallucination in LVLMs
L64: Although LVLMs, built upon open-source language models such as LLaMA [cite43†28 ] and Vicuna [cite44†6 ], have achieved effective multimodal fusion of text and images—greatly enhancing cross-modal understanding and generation—they still face a critical challenge: hallucination. Hallucination refers to factual inconsistencies between the generated text and the input image, often manifesting as fabricated objects or inaccurate descriptions of attributes and relationships.
L65: This issue primarily stems from two key factors: (1) an over-reliance on prior knowledge (e.g., biases in training data or inherent language priors within LLMs) [cite26†19 , cite27†13 ], and (2) limitations in visual encoder localization, misalignment of multimodal information [cite28†27 , cite33†34 ], or suboptimal attention modeling during decoding [cite30†37 , cite31†29 ], all of which hinder accurate associations between inputs and outputs.
L66: ### 2.2 Hallucination Mitigation in LVLMs
L67: LVLM hallucination mitigation falls into two categories: training-based and training-free post-processing methods.
L68: Training-based approaches include: MIA-DPO [cite26†19 ] creates multi-image data via grid collages from single inputs; LLaVA-RLHF [cite28†27 ] reduces reward hacking through fact-grounded reward models; DAMA [cite34†20 ] dynamically optimizes training by aligning data difficulty with response strategies; PerturboLLAVA [cite35†4 ] weakens language prior reliance via adversarial text perturbations; EACO [cite33†34 ] achieves alignment with only 5,000 images (high efficiency); AMP [cite45†38 ] enhances fine-grained recognition through multi-level preference optimization.
L69: Training-free post-processing improves generation accuracy without retraining. These methods fall into two categories: contrastive decoding [cite27†13 , cite29†9 , cite36†7 , cite37†35 ] and attention reallocation [cite30†37 , cite37†35 , cite31†29 , cite46†39 ]. Contrastive decoding reduces bias via cross-image comparison (VCD [cite27†13 ]), uses penalties/rollback for refinement (OPERA [cite29†9 ]), and strengthens vision-language alignment (M3ID [cite36†7 ]).
L70: Attention reallocation improves responsiveness with blind-token calibration (AVISC [cite37†35 ]), provides plug-and-play enhancement (ClearSight [cite30†37 ]), optimizes resource use through recycling (AttnReal [cite31†29 ]), and broadcasts attention matrices for focus enhancement (EAH [cite46†39 ]), achieving minimal computational overhead.
L71: Our approach fundamentally differs by leveraging inference-time intervention [cite47†15 , cite48†5 ]. The core procedure comprises: (1) identifying factual-response directions in activation space; (2) steering activation vectors toward these directions during reasoning. Compared to existing methods, this technique offers: negligible computational overhead; strong interpretability through explicit activation manipulation; and significant hallucination reduction while preserving generation quality.
L72: ## 3 Methodology
L73: ### 3.1 Background and Notation
L74: The input to an LVLM is multimodal, consisting of both text and visual components. For simplicity, we do not distinguish between system prompts and user instructions in text input. Let ${\mathbf{x}}^{t}=\{x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{M}\}$ denote the token sequence encoded by the text encoder, representing a textual feature sequence of length $M$. Similarly, let ${\mathbf{x}}^{v}=\{x_{M+1},x_{M+2},\dots,x_{M+N}\}$ represent the visual token sequence encoded by the vision encoder, with length $N$.
L75: These sequences are concatenated into a unified input sequence ${\mathbf{x}}=\{x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{M+N}\}$ before being fed into the LVLM.
L76: The LVLM processes this input token sequence ${\mathbf{x}}$ through $L$ Transformer layers. The output at layer $l+1$, denoted as ${\mathbf{h}}^{(l+1)}$, is computed using multi-head self-attention [cite38†31 ]:
L77: 
L78:  | $${\mathbf{h}}^{(l+1)}={\mathbf{h}}^{(l)}+\sum_{n=1}^{N_{h}}\mathcal{A}_{n}^{l}({\mathbf{h}}^{(l)}){\bm{P}}_{n}^{l}$$  |  | (1)
L79: where $N_{h}$ is the total number of heads in a Transformer layer, $d$ is the dimension of the operation space of each head; $\mathcal{A}_{n}^{l}(\cdot)$ is the attention operator of the $n$-th head in the $l$-th layer; ${\bm{P}}_{n}^{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times dN_{h}}$ maps the activated output of attention head back to the operation space of the $l$-th Transformer layer. Each layer performs self-attention to capture interactions between text and visual features.
L80: At the final layer (layer $L$), the hidden states ${\mathbf{h}}^{(L)}$ are passed through an affine layer to produce logits of size equal to the vocabulary, followed by Softmax normalization to generate the probability distribution for the next token $y_{t}$:
L81:  | $$p(y_{t}|y_{<t})=\text{Softmax}(\text{Affine}({\mathbf{h}}_{t}^{(L)}))$$  |
L82: 
L83: where $y_{<t}$ represents the previously generated tokens.
L84: ### 3.2 Modeling Trusted and Hallucinated Manifolds of Attention Activations
L85: Our method, Scalpel, is based on two key findings from studies on LLMs and LVLMs. First, these models often know the correct answer but fail to express it clearly [cite49†32 , cite50†11 , cite51†24 ]. Second, their activation space has key directions that guide truthful responses [cite52†3 , cite53†26 ]. Earlier methods like ITI [cite47†15 ] and ICT [cite48†5 ] reduce hallucinations by steering attention heads toward trusted activation spaces.
L86: Yet, they use fixed correction directions for all activations in a head, which may not be ideal. Scalpel improves this by customizing intervention directions for different activations during inference, even within the same attention head. To do this efficiently, we separately discretize and tokenize trusted and hallucinated manifolds, then create a one-to-one mapping between them. GMMs [cite54†23 ] are well-suited for this task.
L87: We use GMMs to model attention activations from both trusted and hallucinated data, approximating their manifolds.
L88: Trusted activation manifolds are built from valid (image, Q, A) triplets (e.g., Q: What is in the image? A: Zebra.), extracting attention activations across all layers/heads, as shown in the left part of Figure cite39†1 . Hallucinated manifolds are created by perturbing images while keeping QA pairs correct, or by altering specific image regions (e.g., zebra bounding boxes).
L89: For each attention head/layer, we extract three manifolds: (1) trusted, (2) image-perturbed hallucinated, (3) object-perturbed hallucinated. Activation distributions differ slightly between image and object levels, leading to different intervention directions. At the object level, as shown in Figure cite55†2 , most background remains unchanged, so only specific components need large-scale intervention. However, intervention principles are identical, so we omit level distinctions in the following.
L90: Our goal is to map hallucinated to trusted manifolds, enabling corrective adjustments to activations. Let $\rho_{H}$ denote the hallucinated manifold’s distribution with sample ${\mathbf{z}}_{0}$, and $\rho_{T}$ the trusted with ${\mathbf{z}}_{1}$. The optimal mapping is given by the entropic optimal transport (EOT) problem [cite56†21 ]:
L91:  | $$\min_{\pi\in\Pi(\rho_{H},\rho_{T})}\int\|{\mathbf{z}}_{0}-{\mathbf{z}}_{1}\|^{2}d\pi-\epsilon h(\pi)$$  |  | (2)
L92: 
L93: where $\pi({\mathbf{z}}_{0},{\mathbf{z}}_{1})$ is the transport plan (coupling), $\Pi(\rho_{H},\rho_{T})$ the joint distributions with these marginals, and $h$ the differential entropy:
L94: 
L95:  | $$h(\rho)\triangleq-\int\rho({\mathbf{z}})\log\rho({\mathbf{z}})d{\mathbf{z}}.$$  |  | (3)
L96: Directly solving the EOT problem (Eq. cite57†2 ) from hallucinatory to trusted activations is difficult. Leonard reformulates it as an equivalent Schrödinger bridge problem (SBP) for tractability [cite58†14 ]:
L97:  |  | $\displaystyle\min_{{\mathbf{u}}\in\mathcal{U}}\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\rho_{t}}\left[\int_{0}^{1}\frac{1}{2\epsilon}\|{\mathbf{u}}_{t}({\mathbf{z}}_{t})\|^{2}\,dt\right],$  |  | (4)
L98:  |  | $\displaystyle d{\mathbf{z}}_{t}={\mathbf{u}}_{t}({\mathbf{z}}_{t})\,dt+\sqrt{\epsilon}\,d{\mathbf{w}}_{t},$  |  | (5)
L99:  |  | $\displaystyle{\mathbf{z}}_{0}\sim\rho_{H},\quad{\mathbf{z}}_{1}\sim\rho_{T},$  |  | (6)
L100: where $\mathcal{U}$ is the set of adapted finite-energy controls (i.e., drift in diffusion/SDEs). The goal is minimal-energy control ensuring initial distribution $\rho_{H}$ and terminal $\rho_{T}$. We solve this SBP to derive the optimal path from hallucination to trusted manifolds.
L101: ### 3.3 Optimal Transport Mapping Between Trusted and Hallucinated GMMs
L102: Both trusted/hallucinated GMMs contain multiple components. When an activation belongs to a hallucinated component, mapping requires not only identifying its trusted counterpart but determining the optimal transformation path. To address this, we propose an alignment algorithm superior to random matching. Intuitively, minimal corrections preserve data manifold integrity, thus we seek minimum-cost flow mapping between components, as depicted in the right part of Figure cite39†1 .
L103: This process is performed independently per attention head. Let hallucinated GMM (for a head) be:
L104: 
L105:  | $${\mathbf{z}}_{0}\sim\rho_{H}\approx\sum_{i=1}^{N_{0}}w^{i}_{0}\mathcal{N}(\mu^{i}_{0},\Sigma^{i}_{0}),$$  |  | (7)
L106: 
L107: and trusted GMM:
L108: 
L109:  | $${\mathbf{z}}_{1}\sim\rho_{T}\approx\sum_{j=1}^{N_{1}}w^{j}_{1}\mathcal{N}(\mu^{j}_{1},\Sigma^{j}_{1}),$$  |  | (8)
L110: where $\mu^{i}_{0},\Sigma^{i}_{0}$ and $\mu^{j}_{1},\Sigma^{j}_{1}$ are mean/covariance parameters $\forall i,j$. ${\mathbf{z}}_{0}$, ${\mathbf{z}}_{1}$ represent hallucinated/trusted activations.
L111: 
L112: Eq. (cite59†4 ) becomes optimal mapping between GMMs:
L113: 
L114:  | $$\min_{{\mathbf{u}}\in\mathcal{U}}\mathbb{E}\left[\int_{0}^{1}\|{\mathbf{u}}_{t}({\mathbf{z}})\|^{2}dt\right],\quad d{\mathbf{z}}={\mathbf{u}}_{t}({\mathbf{z}})dt+\sqrt{\epsilon}d{\mathbf{w}}$$  |  | (9)
L115: with ${\mathbf{z}}_{0}$, ${\mathbf{z}}_{1}$ satisfying Eq. (cite60†7 ) and (cite61†8 ).
L116: 
L117: cite62†Image: Refer to caption (a) This comparison shows the distribution of trusted and hallucinated attention activations at the image level. The activations are extracted from the 73rd attention head in layer 1 of LLaVA-1.5. As seen in the middle image, most of the eight hallucination components require noticeable interventions, varying in both direction and scale.
L118: cite63†Image: Refer to caption (b) This comparison shows distributions of trusted and hallucinated object-level attention activations, illustrating GMM transitions. Activations originate from LLaVA-1.5’s 12th layer, 62nd head. Of 8 hallucinated components, 6-7 require minimal intervention while the rightmost needs substantial correction. This occurs because hallucinations typically affect single bounding boxes, leaving other image areas intact - as shown in Figure cite39†1 (zebra example).
L119: Figure 2: Comparison of the distributions of trusted and hallucinated attention activations at the image and object levels, including the component composition of GMMs and the transition flow from the hallucinated GMMs to the trusted GMMs. For simplicity, we use t-SNE [cite64†30 ] to map the original attention activations into a two-dimensional space before performing all subsequent operations.
L120: This formulation physically avoids excessive perturbation of LVLM attention activations, preserving visual-language alignment. Instead, it achieves hallucinated-to-trusted state shifts via minimal integral intervention.
L121: The Schrödinger bridge solves optimal transport between distributions. Bunne et al. [cite65†2 ] derived a closed-form Gaussian bridge solution for single Gaussians (i.e., $N_{0}=N_{1}=1$ in Eq. cite66†9 constrained by Eq. cite60†7 ,cite61†8 ). This yields the most probable diffusion path under Brownian motion, with Gaussian marginals at all times. Rapakoulias et al. [cite67†22 ] extended this to GMMs by combining Gaussian bridges via linear programming, ensuring theoretical feasibility and performance bounds.
L122: Thus, we establish optimal hallucination-to-trust GMM mapping (Proposition 1), maximally preserving LVLM vision-language alignment.
L123: ###### Proposition 3.1.
L124: 
L125: (Optimal GMM bridge policy for hallucination-to-trust transition) For the GMM bridge problem in Eq. (cite66†9 ), let ${\mathbf{u}}_{t|ij}$ be the optimal control policy for the Gaussian bridge between hallucinated component $i$ ($\mathcal{N}(\mu_{0}^{i},\Sigma_{0}^{i})$) and trusted component $j$ ($\mathcal{N}(\mu_{1}^{j},\Sigma_{1}^{j})$), with induced flow $\rho_{t|ij}$ and optimal cost $J_{ij}$.
L126: 
L127: Consider this linear program:
L128: 
L129:  | $$\min_{\lambda}\sum_{i,j}\lambda_{ij}J_{ij}$$  |
L130: under optimal transport constraints with $\lambda_{ij}\geq 0,\forall i,j$:
L131: 
L132:  | $\displaystyle\sum_{j=1}^{N_{1}}\lambda_{ij}$  | $\displaystyle=w_{0}^{i},$  | $\displaystyle i$  | $\displaystyle=1,\dots,N_{0},$  |
L133:  | $\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{N_{0}}\lambda_{ij}$  | $\displaystyle=w_{1}^{j},$  | $\displaystyle j$  | $\displaystyle=1,\dots,N_{1}.$  |
L134: 
L135: The solution $\lambda_{ij}^{*}$ gives the optimal lower bound for Eq. (cite66†9 ). The optimal mixture policy:
L136:  | $${\mathbf{u}}_{t}({\mathbf{z}})=\sum_{i,j}{\mathbf{u}}_{t|ij}({\mathbf{z}})\frac{\rho_{t|ij}({\mathbf{z}})\lambda_{ij}^{*}}{\sum_{r,\ell}\rho_{t|r\ell}({\mathbf{z}})\lambda_{r\ell}^{*}},$$  |  | (10)
L137: 
L138: is feasible for (cite66†9 ) with induced flow:
L139: 
L140:  | $$\rho_{t}({\mathbf{z}})=\sum_{i,j}\rho_{t|ij}({\mathbf{z}})\lambda_{ij}^{*}.$$  |
L141: Proof sketch. For system $d{\mathbf{z}}_{t}={\mathbf{u}}({\mathbf{z}}_{t})dt+\sqrt{\epsilon}d{\mathbf{w}}_{t}$, the Fokker–Planck–Kolmogorov (FPK) [cite68†12 ] equation uniquely determines $\rho_{t}$’s evolution and enables control policy derivation. The parabolic PDE guarantees existence/uniqueness of $\rho_{t}$, forming the basis for state-feedback control minimizing quadratic costs. Additive Gaussian noise and linear dynamics ensure tractability via Riccati equations, yielding closed-form optimal policies.
L142: ∎
L143: Per Proposition cite69†3.1 , the solution uses a mixture strategy weighting conditional policies by $\lambda_{ij}^{*}\rho_{t|ij}({\mathbf{z}})$, normalized by $\sum_{i,j}\rho_{t|ij}({\mathbf{z}})\lambda_{ij}^{*}$. Since $\rho_{t|ij}({\mathbf{z}})$ is Gaussian centered at the $(i,j)$-bridge mean at $t$, this prioritizes strategies with means closer to ${\mathbf{z}}$. Applied independently per attention head, the Schrödinger bridge framework maps hallucinated to trusted Gaussian components with minimal cost.
L144: Here $\lambda_{ij}^{*}$ represents transport weight from hallucinated $i$ to trusted $j$. For each $i$, select $j^{*}=\arg\max_{j}\lambda_{ij}^{*}$ as the trusted counterpart for mitigation (used next section). Figure cite55†2 shows optimal transfer examples from hallucination to trusted manifold. These cases, from different attention heads, demonstrate both image-level and object-level interventions. We observe that tailored interventions can be applied for distinct tokens, enabling greater customization.
L145: ### 3.4 Hallucination Mitigation Based on Optimal Mapping Between Manifolds
L146: To identify critical attention heads, we train logistic regression probes over all $L$ layers and $N_{h}$ heads of the vision-language transformer. Given activation tensors $\mathcal{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times L\times N_{h}\times d}$ (batch size $B$, dimension $d$), we train a logistic model on each head’s activations to distinguish hallucinated (1) vs factual (0) inputs using cross-entropy loss with $L_{2}$-regularized weights.
L147: The accuracy matrix $\mathcal{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times N_{h}}$ reveals top-$k$ heads for intervention analysis (Figure cite39†1 ), where dark orange highlights indicate strong hallucination discrimination capacity.
L148: The mitigation strategy dynamically adjusts key attention heads during answer generation to influence outputs. At each step, we extract the current token’s high-dimensional attention vector, reflecting the model’s internal state critical for next-word prediction in autoregressive models. Using pre-trained hallucinated and trusted GMMs (with multiple components), and their optimal mapping (Section cite13†3.3 ), we analyze activation distributions to determine most probable component membership.
L149: This clustering interprets activation roles by comparing positions against learned patterns. Let ${\mathbf{z}}_{\text{current}}=\mathbf{z}_{t}^{(l,h)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$ denote the output activation vector at step $t$ from layer $l$, head $h$. Compute posterior probabilities for hallucation GMM components:
L150:  | $$\mathcal{P}(r|\mathbf{z})=\frac{w^{r}_{0}\mathcal{N}({\mathbf{z}}|\mu^{r}_{0},\Sigma^{r}_{0})}{\sum_{i=1}^{N_{0}}w^{i}_{0}\mathcal{N}({\mathbf{z}}|\mu^{i}_{0},\Sigma^{i}_{0})}.$$  |  | (11)
L151: 
L152: Let
L153: 
L154:  | $$r^{*}=\underset{r}{\arg\max}\ \mathcal{P}(r\mid{\mathbf{z}}_{\text{current}})$$  |  | (12)
L155: 
L156: denote the hallucinated Gaussian component to which the current activation ${\mathbf{z}}_{\text{current}}$ is most likely assigned, and let
L157:  | $$c=\max_{r}\mathcal{P}(r\mid{\mathbf{z}}_{\text{current}})$$  |  | (13)
L158: 
L159: represent the corresponding assignment probability.
L160: 
L161: The intervention uses precomputed Schrödinger bridge mappings (Section cite13†3.3 ). When identifying a hallucinated component via Eq. (cite70†12 ), we retrieve its trusted counterpart through $\lambda_{ij}^{*}$ in Eq. (cite71†10 ) and compute the transfer vector as ${\mathbf{v}}_{r^{*}}=\bm{\mu}_{1}^{j^{*}}-\mu_{0}^{r^{*}}$, where $j^{*}=\argmax_{j}\lambda_{r^{*}j}$.
L162: Intervention strength combines base coefficient $\alpha_{\text{base}}$ and Eq. (cite72†13 ) confidence:
L163: 
L164:  | $$\alpha_{\text{dynamic}}=\alpha_{\text{base}}\cdot c$$  |  | (14)
L165: 
L166: Strong matches (c$\approx$1) get stronger interventions, while ambiguous cases receive milder adjustments to maintain stability. Apply the scaled vector:
L167:  | $${\mathbf{h}}^{(l+1)}={\mathbf{h}}^{(l)}+\sum_{n=1}^{N_{h}}\left[\mathcal{A}_{n}^{l}({\mathbf{h}}^{(l)})+\mathbf{1}_{\text{top-}k}(l,n)\alpha_{\text{dynamic}}{\mathbf{v}}_{r^{*}}\right]{\bm{P}}_{n}^{l}$$  |  | (15)
L168: Only top-$k$ heads (Figure cite39†1 ) get modified via indicator function $\mathbf{1}_{\text{top-}k}$, ensuring minimal perturbation while guiding activations toward trusted distributions. This correction applies at each generation step to reduce hallucinations while preserving generation coherence.
